Creating a simple and minimal init program in Rust without any dependencies on dbus, systemvinit, systemd, elogind, x11, xorg, xinit, systemctl, or nix might look like this:

First, let's create a `Cargo.toml` file to define our project and dependencies:

```toml
[package]
name = "sysx"
version = "0.1.0"
authors = ["Your Name <you@example.com>"]
edition = "2018"

[dependencies]
```

Now, we can create a Rust script `src/main.rs` for our init program:

```rust
use std::fs;
use std::process::Command;
use std::io::{BufRead, BufReader};

const TASKS_FILE: &str = "/etc/sysx/tasks";
const SHELL: &str = "/bin/sh";

fn main() {
    let task_file = match fs::File::open(TASKS_FILE) {
        Ok(file) => file,
        Err(_) => {
            eprintln!("Error: Could not open task file '{}'", TASKS_FILE);
            return;
        }
    };

    let tasks = BufReader::new(task_file);

    for task_line in tasks.lines() {
        let task = match task_line {
            Ok(line) => line,
            Err(_) => {
                eprintln!("Error: Could not read task file '{}'", TASKS_FILE);
                return;
            }
        };

        // Skip empty lines and comments
        if task.is_empty() || task.trim().starts_with("#") {
            continue;
        }

        match run_task(&task) {
            Ok(status) if status.success() => println!("Task '{}' success", task),
            Ok(status) => println!("Task '{}' failed with exit code: {}", task, status.code().unwrap_or(-1)),
            Err(e) => println!("Task '{}' could not be started: {}", task, e),
        }
    }
}

fn run_task(task: &str) -> Result<std::process::ExitStatus, std::io::Error> {
    Command::new(SHELL).arg("-c").arg(task).status()
}
```

This program reads tasks from the `/etc/sysx/tasks` file and executes them in the order they appear in the file. Comments and empty lines are ignored. Tasks are expected to be shell commands. Each task's output will be displayed, and its exit status will be shown upon completion.

To build and run the project, use the following commands:

```
cargo build
cargo run
```

For this program to be useful, you will need to create the `/etc/sysx/tasks` file on your Devuan machine and add the tasks you want to execute in their proper sequence.

