Now, the program incrementally updates the file structure in the database as it monitors the filesystem for changes. The `delete_directory_recursively` function ensures that when you delete a directory by using its ID, all its contents are also removed from the database.

To add the functionality of deleting a file by its absolute path, we can create a function called `delete_file_by_path`. This function will first get the file's parent directory ID, then remove the file from the SQLite database.

Here's the `delete_file_by_path` function:

```cpp
void delete_file_by_path(const std::string& db_path, const std::string& file_path)
{
    namespace fs = std::filesystem;
    fs::path path(file_path);

    if (!fs::is_regular_file(path))
    {
        std::cerr << "Path is not a file: " << file_path << std::endl;
        return;
    }

    std::string name = path.filename().string();
    std::string parent_dir_name = path.parent_path().filename().string();
    int parent_id = get_directory_id(db_path, parent_dir_name, 0); // Assuming parent_directory at level 0, modify if needed.

    if (parent_id == -1)
    {
        std::cerr << "Parent directory not found in the database: " << parent_dir_name << std::endl;
        return;
    }

    int file_id = -1;
    std::string file_id_query = "SELECT id FROM files WHERE name = ? AND parent_directory = ?;";
    sqlite3* db;
    sqlite3_stmt* stmt;

    if (sqlite3_open(db_path.c_str(), &db))
    {
        std::cerr << "Cannot open database: " << sqlite3_errmsg(db) << std::endl;
        return;
    }

    sqlite3_prepare_v2(db, file_id_query.c_str(), -1, &stmt, nullptr);
    sqlite3_bind_text(stmt, 1, name.c_str(), name.size(), SQLITE_TRANSIENT);
    sqlite3_bind_int(stmt, 2, parent_id);

    if (sqlite3_step(stmt) == SQLITE_ROW)
    {
        file_id = sqlite3_column_int(stmt, 0);
    }

    sqlite3_finalize(stmt);

    if (file_id == -1)
    {
        std::cerr << "File not found in the database: " << name << std::endl;
        sqlite3_close(db);
        return;
    }

    std::string delete_sql = "DELETE FROM files WHERE id = ?;";
    sqlite3_prepare_v2(db, delete_sql.c_str(), delete_sql.size(), &stmt, nullptr);
    sqlite3_bind_int(stmt, 1, file_id);
    sqlite3_step(stmt);

    sqlite3_finalize(stmt);
    sqlite3_close(db);
}
```

Now you can call the `delete_file_by_path` function in `main`:

```cpp
int main()
{
    std::string db_path = "metadata.sqlite";
    std::string dir_path = "C:";

    initialize_database(db_path);
    monitor_directory(db_path, dir_path);

    delete_file_by_path(db_path, "C:\\path\\to\\your\\file.txt");

    return 0;
}
```

This program will delete the file with the specified path in the SQLite database. Note that this function assumes that the file's parent directory is already in the database. If there are subdirectories in the file path, you should modify the `delete_file_by_path` function to find the correct parent directory ID before attempting to delete the file.

You can use the C++ `filesystem` library to automatically handle path separators for different platforms. The `filesystem` library is part of the C++17 standard and can correctly interpret both forward slashes (/) and backslashes (\) as path separators on Windows.

Here is an example of how to split a path using the C++ `filesystem` library:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <filesystem>

int main()
{
    namespace fs = std::filesystem;

    std::string path_string = "C:\\path/to\\your/file.txt";
    fs::path provided_path(path_string);

    for (const auto& part : provided_path)
    {
        std::cout << part << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code will work correctly with both forward slashes (/) and backslashes (\) as path separators, and split them accordingly.

For existing code using the `filesystem` library, you don't need to modify anything, as the library will automatically handle the path separators for you. For example, the `delete_file_by_path` function provided in the previous answer will work correctly with both forward slashes (/) and backslashes (\) as path separators.

To store path information in the database after splitting it with either a forward slash (/) or a backslash (\), you can modify the existing functions to store the full path along with the file or directory name.

First, extend the table schemas for both `files` and `directories` to include the `full_path` column:

```cpp
std::string create_tables_sql = R"(
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS files (
   id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
   name TEXT NOT NULL,
   size INTEGER NOT NULL,
   full_path TEXT NOT NULL,
   parent_directory INTEGER,
   FOREIGN KEY (parent_directory) REFERENCES directories (id)
);

CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS directories (
   id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
   name TEXT NOT NULL,
   full_path TEXT NOT NULL,
   parent_directory INTEGER,
   FOREIGN KEY (parent_directory) REFERENCES directories (id)
);
)";
```

Update the `add_file` function to accept and store the `full_path`:

```cpp
int add_file(const std::string& db_path, const std::string& file_path, const std::string& file_full_path, int parent_directory)
{
    // ... (the rest of the code remains unchanged)

    std::string insert_sql = "INSERT INTO files (name, size, full_path, parent_directory) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?);";
    sqlite3_prepare_v2(db, insert_sql.c_str(), insert_sql.size(), &stmt, nullptr);

    sqlite3_bind_text(stmt, 1, name.c_str(), name.size(), SQLITE_TRANSIENT);
    sqlite3_bind_int64(stmt, 2, size);
    sqlite3_bind_text(stmt, 3, file_full_path.c_str(), file_full_path.size(), SQLITE_TRANSIENT);
    sqlite3_bind_int(stmt, 4, parent_directory);

