To save a Blob file to local disk on a server, you will need a server-side scripting language like Node.js, Python, or PHP. This is because JavaScript alone cannot access the filesystem on a server, and a Blob object only exists on the client-side.

Here is an example using Node.js with the `fs` (filesystem) module:

1. First, install Node.js if you haven't already: https://nodejs.org/en/download/
2. Create a new folder and navigate to it in the terminal.
3. Run `npm init` and follow the instructions to create a `package.json` file.
4. Create an `app.js` file in the same folder as your `package.json` file, and add the following code:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const multer = require('multer');
const fs = require('fs');

// Set up server
const app = express();
const port = process.env.PORT || 3000;

// Set up file upload middleware
const upload = multer({ storage: multer.memoryStorage() });

// Route for uploading the file in Blob format
app.post('/upload', upload.single('file'), (req, res) => {
  if (!req.file) {
    return res.status(400).send("No file uploaded");
  }

  const filePath = `./saved-files/${req.file.originalname}`;

  // Write the file to disk
  fs.writeFile(filePath, req.file.buffer, (err) => {
    if (err) {
      console.error(err);
      return res.status(500).send("Error saving the file");
    }
    res.send("File saved successfully");
  });
});

