The issue occurs because some of the files in the vault do not have a `basename` property. This can happen for various reasons, such as special files, system files, or simply missing metadata. To avoid this issue, you can add a check to ensure that the `version` object in the forEach loop of the `getVersions()` method has a `basename` property before using it.

Update the `getVersions()` method with a check like this:

```javascript
async getVersions(file: TFile) {
  const folder = this.settings.versionsLocation;
  const versions: TFile[] = [];

  if (folder) {
    this.app.vault.getMarkdownFiles(this.settings.versionsLocation).forEach((version) => {
      // Check if version has a basename property
      if (version.hasOwnProperty("basename") && version.basename.startsWith(file.basename)) {
        versions.push(version);
      }
    });

    versions.sort((a: TFile, b: TFile) => {
      const timestampA = parseInt(
        a.basename.split("-").slice(-1).toString()
      );
      const timestampB = parseInt(
        b.basename.split("-").slice(-1).toString()
      );

      return timestampA - timestampB;
    });
  }

  return versions;
}
```

With this check in place, the plugin should not encounter the TypeError while reading the `basename` property. The file version view will now display the file versions as expected.

