```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

void print_permutations(int n) {
    std::vector<int> values(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        values[i] = i;
    }
    do {
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            std::cout << values[i] << " ";
        }
        std::cout << std::endl;
    } while (std::next_permutation(values.begin(), values.end()));
}

int main() {
    int n = 4;
    print_permutations(n);
    return 0;
}
```

This function generates all permutations of an n-length array of integers that contain values between 0 and n-1. You can adjust the range of values by modifying the initialization of the `values` vector.

The function uses a loop to initialize the `values` vector with the numbers 0 to n-1. It then uses the `next_permutation` function to generate all possible permutations, printing each one as it goes.

Note that this function runs in O(n!) time, because there are n! possible permutations of an n-length array. For large values of n, this can become very slow.

