Creating a simple JPEG decoder from scratch without using any external libraries can be quite a complex task, as you would need to handle various aspects of the JPEG standard, such as Huffman coding, quantization tables, and more.

However, it can be a great learning experience if you're interested in delving deeper into JPEG image format and low-level programming. The following code provides a basic outline for a simple C JPEG decoder without using any external libraries. Note that this code is only a starting point, and does not handle all aspects of the JPEG standard. It's highly recommended to use an external library for more robust and efficient handling.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

// Make sure to replace this with the actual path to your JPEG image file.
const char *filename = "path_to_a_jpeg_image_file.jpg";

typedef struct {
    unsigned short SOI;
    unsigned short APP0;
    unsigned short DQT;
    unsigned short SOF0;
    unsigned short DHT;
    unsigned short SOS;
    unsigned short EOI;
    unsigned short COM;
} JPEGMarkers;

void read_header(FILE *file);
void decode_image(FILE *file);
void process_image();

int main() {
    FILE *file;

    file = fopen(filename, "rb");
    if (!file) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to open file: %s\n", filename);
        exit(1);
    }

    read_header(file);
    decode_image(file);
    process_image();
    fclose(file);

    return 0;
}

void read_header(FILE *file) {
    printf("Reading JPEG headers...\n");
    // Read and process JPEG headers (e.g., SOI, APP0, DQT, SOF0, DHT, SOS, EOI, COM).
}

