    // Read the encoded image data from the file and perform decoding operations (e.g., Huffman decoding, dequantization, IDCT, etc.).
}

void process_image() {
    printf("Processing the decoded image...\n");
    // Process the decoded image data (e.g., color space conversion, writing to a BMP file, etc.).
}
```

Please note that the above code provides just the skeleton and does not perform actual decoding operations. To actually decode a JPEG image, you would need to handle a multitude of tasks, such as:

1. Parsing JPEG markers (e.g., SOI, APP0, DQT, SOF0, DHT, SOS, EOI, COM).
2. Supporting Huffman decoding for lossless compression.
3. Implementing inverse discrete cosine transform (IDCT) for lossy compression.
4. Understanding and handling the different color spaces (e.g., YCbCr, YCCK, RGB).
5. Generating the final bitmap image in a common format (e.g., BMP).

For a more comprehensive understanding of JPEG decoding, you can refer to the official [JPEG specification](https://www.w3.org/Graphics/JPEG/itu-t81.pdf) and study various resources and tutorials available online. However, I highly recommend using an established C library like libjpeg for actual projects that require JPEG decoding, as it is robust, efficient, and well-maintained.

