The Traveling Salesperson Problem (TSP) is an optimization problem that tries to find the shortest possible route that visits a given set of cities and returns to the origin city. Solving the TSP using Dynamic Programming involves using a technique called "memoization" to store intermediate solutions and reusing them to avoid redundant computations.

Here's a Java program that uses dynamic programming to solve the TSP:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class TravelingSalesperson {
    private int n; // number of cities
    private int[][] distance; // distance table
    private int[][] memo; // memo table for dynamic programming

    public TravelingSalesperson(int[][] distance) {
        this.distance = distance;
        this.n = distance.length;
        this.memo = new int[n][(1 << n) - 1];
    }

    public int solveTSP() {
        for (int[] row : memo) {
            Arrays.fill(row, -1); // Initialize memo table with -1
        }
        return tsp(0, 1); // start with the first city (0) being visited
    }

    private int tsp(int currentPosition, int visitedMask) {
        if (visitedMask == (1 << n) - 1) {
            return distance[currentPosition][0]; // return distance to origin city
        }

        if (memo[currentPosition][visitedMask] != -1) {
            return memo[currentPosition][visitedMask]; // return cached result
        }

        int minDistance = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        for (int nextCity = 0; nextCity < n; nextCity++) {
            if ((visitedMask & (1 << nextCity)) == 0) { // check if nextCity has not been visited
                int distanceToNext = distance[currentPosition][nextCity] + tsp(nextCity, visitedMask | (1 << nextCity));
                minDistance = Math.min(minDistance, distanceToNext);
            }
        }

        memo[currentPosition][visitedMask] = minDistance;
        return minDistance;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] distance = {
            {0, 10, 15, 20},
            {10, 0, 35, 25},
            {15, 35, 0, 30},
            {20, 25, 30, 0}
        };

        TravelingSalesperson tsp = new TravelingSalesperson(distance);
        System.out.println("Shortest Route: " + tsp.solveTSP());
    }
}
```

This program initializes a 2D distance array, which represents the distance between each pair of cities. The `TravelingSalesperson` class constructor takes this distance array and initializes the necessary data structures for the dynamic programming approach. 

The `solveTSP()` method initializes the memo table with -1 and calls the `tsp()` function with the starting city (0) and visited mask (1). The `tsp()` function is a recursive function that explores all possible routes and returns the shortest path.

In the `main()` method, a 4x4 distance matrix is created, and an object of the `TravelingSalesperson` class is initialized with this matrix. The shortest route is then computed and printed.

For this program, I will assume you are familiar with the traveling salesperson problem (TSP) and have a basic understanding of backtracking algorithms. In this particular code, we are using adjacency matrix representation for the graph.

Here is a Java program for solving TSP using the backtracking algorithm:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class TSPBacktracking {

    static int N, start;
    static boolean[] visited;
    static int[] path;
    static int[][] graph;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        N = 4; // number of vertices in the graph
        start = 0; // assume the starting vertex is 0

        graph = new int[][] { // adjacency matrix representation of the graph
            {0, 10, 15, 20},
            {10, 0, 35, 25},
            {15, 35, 0, 30},
            {20, 25, 30, 0}
        };

        visited = new boolean[N];
        path = new int[N + 1];

        visited[start] = true;
        path[0] = start;

        tsp(start, 1);

        System.out.println("The shortest path is: " + Arrays.toString(path));

    }

    static int minCost = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

    static void tsp(int v, int count) {
        if (count == N) {
            int cost = graph[v][start];
            if (cost < minCost) {
                minCost = cost;
                for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
                    path[i] = path[i];
                }
                path[N] = start;
            }
            return;
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            if (i != v && !visited[i]) {
                int cost = graph[v][i];
                if (minCost - cost > 0) {
                    visited[i] = true;
                    path[count] = i;
                    minCost -= cost;

                    tsp(i, count + 1);

                    visited[i] = false;
                    minCost += cost;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This Java program defines a function called `tsp()`, which takes the current city(v) and the count of visited cities as parameters. The function uses recursion and backtracking to solve the TSP problem. Once it has found a possible tour, it will compare with the previous tours and updates the minimum cost and path accordingly. After all recursive calls, the final path will be printed in the main() function.

Please note that this code may not be efficient for large graphs, as the time complexity for solving the TSP problem using the backtracking algorithm can be exponential in the worst case.

The Traveling Salesperson Problem (TSP) is an optimization problem that aims to find the shortest possible route that visits a given set of cities and returns to the starting city. The Branch and Bound algorithm can be used to efficiently find an optimal solution to the TSP. The following Java program demonstrates how to solve the TSP using the Branch and Bound algorithm.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

class Node implements Comparable<Node> {
    int level;
    int pathCost;
    int bound;
    int[] path;

    Node(int level, int pathCost, int bound, int[] path) {
        this.level = level;
        this.pathCost = pathCost;
        this.bound = bound;
        this.path = path;
    }

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Node other) {
        return this.bound - other.bound;
    }
}

public class TravelingSalesperson {
    private static int[][] adjacencyMatrix;
    private static int n;
    private static int minLength;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        adjacencyMatrix = new int[][] {
                {0, 10, 15, 20},
                {10, 0, 35, 25},
                {15, 35, 0, 30},
                {20, 25, 30, 0}
        };
        n = adjacencyMatrix.length;
        minLength = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        System.out.println("Shortest route length: " + solveTSP());
    }

    private static int solveTSP() {
        PriorityQueue<Node> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();
        int[] initialPath = new int[n + 1];
        Arrays.fill(initialPath, -1);

        Node v = new Node(0, 0, lowerBound(initialPath), initialPath);
        pq.add(v);

        while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
            v = pq.poll();

            if (v.bound < minLength) {
                for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
                    if (!contains(v.path, i)) {
                        int[] newPath = Arrays.copyOf(v.path, n + 1);
                        newPath[v.level + 1] = i;
                        int pathCost = v.pathCost + adjacencyMatrix[v.path[v.level]][i];

                        if (v.level == n - 2) {
                            newPath[n] = 0;
                            pathCost += adjacencyMatrix[i][0];
                            if (pathCost < minLength) {
                                minLength = pathCost;
                            }
                        } else {
                            int newBound = v.bound - lowerBound(newPath);
                            if (pathCost + newBound < minLength) {
                                pq.add(new Node(v.level + 1, pathCost, newBound, newPath));
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return minLength;
    }

    private static int lowerBound(int[] path) {
        boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];
        for (int city : path) {
            if (city != -1) {
                visited[city] = true;
            }
        }

        int bound = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            int minEdge = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (!visited[j] && adjacencyMatrix[i][j] < minEdge) {
                    minEdge = adjacencyMatrix[i][j];
                }
            }
            bound += minEdge;
        }

        int minEdge = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (!visited[i] && adjacencyMatrix[n - 1][i] < minEdge) {
                minEdge = adjacencyMatrix[n - 1][i];
            }
        }
        bound += minEdge;
        return bound;
    }

    private static boolean contains(int[] path, int city) {
        for (int c : path) {
            if (c == city) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
}
