Wake word detection is an important aspect of voice-activated applications and devices. It allows the device to be "woken up" by recognizing a specific word or phrase, without the need for a button press or other input.

Here is an example C++ code for implementing wake word detection:

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

// Function to detect the wake word "Hey Siri"
bool detectWakeWord(std::vector<std::string>& input) {
    int i = 0;
    int len = input.size();
    while (i < len-2) {
        if (input[i] == "Hey" && input[i+1] == "Siri") {
            // Wake word detected
            return true;
        }
        i++;
    }
    // Wake word not detected
    return false;
}

int main() {
    // Sample input
    std::vector<std::string> input = {"This", "is", "a", "test", "phrase", "Hey", "Siri"};

    // Call function to detect wake word
    if (detectWakeWord(input)) {
        std::cout << "Wake word detected!" << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "Wake word not detected." << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `detectWakeWord` function takes in a vector of strings representing the input audio stream. It checks each pair of consecutive strings in the vector for the wake word "Hey Siri". If the wake word is detected, the function returns true. Otherwise, it returns false.

The main function provides a sample input vector and calls the `detectWakeWord` function. If the wake word is detected, it outputs "Wake word detected!". Otherwise, it outputs "Wake word not detected.".

This is a simple example, and in real-world applications, wake word detection can be much more complex and accurate. Advanced techniques like machine learning and signal processing can be used to improve the accuracy and reliability of wake word detection.

