If you set your `UNiagaraSystem` from a Blueprint, you can define a UPROPERTY in your C++ class to hold a reference to the Niagara System. That way, when the Blueprint assigns the Niagara System, you will have access to it in your C++ code. Here's an example:

1. Create a UPROPERTY in your C++ class to hold a reference to the `UNiagaraSystem`:

```cpp
// MyActor.h

#pragma once
#include "CoreMinimal.h"
#include "GameFramework/Actor.h"
#include "NiagaraSystem.h"
#include "MyActor.generated.h"

UCLASS()
class AMyActor : public AActor
{
    GENERATED_BODY()

public:
    // Add a UPROPERTY to hold a reference to the Niagara System
    UPROPERTY(EditAnywhere, BlueprintReadOnly, Category = "Niagara System")
    UNiagaraSystem* NiagaraSystemReference;

    // ...
};
```

2. In your Blueprint based on your created C++ class, assign the desired Niagara System to the `NiagaraSystemReference` property.

3. To create a Niagara component from the Niagara System and utilize user parameters, follow the same steps as in the previous example:

```cpp
// MyActor.cpp

#include "MyActor.h"
#include "MyNiagaraFunctionLibrary.h" // include your custom Niagara Function Library header
#include "NiagaraComponent.h"
#include "Engine/World.h"
#include "Components/SceneComponent.h"

// ...

UFUNCTION()
void MyExampleFunction()
{
    if (NiagaraSystemReference == nullptr)
    {
        // Ensure the Niagara System reference is set
        UE_LOG(LogTemp, Warning, TEXT("NiagaraSystemReference is not set."));
        return;
    }

    // Create and initialize Niagara component using the NiagaraSystemReference
    UNiagaraComponent* NiagaraComponent = NewObject<UNiagaraComponent>(this);
    NiagaraComponent->SetAsset(NiagaraSystemReference);
    NiagaraComponent->SetAutoDestroy(true); // Set AutoDestroy, if required
    NiagaraComponent->RegisterComponentWithWorld(GetWorld());
    NiagaraComponent->AttachToComponent(GetRootComponent(), FAttachmentTransformRules::KeepRelativeTransform);

    // Activate the Niagara system
    NiagaraComponent->ActivateSystem();

    // Set the custom parameter values
    FMyNiagaraCustomParameters CustomParameters;
    CustomParameters.MyCustomFloat = 123.0f;

    // Call the custom Niagara function to set the user parameters
    UMyNiagaraFunctionLibrary::SetMyCustomFloatParameter(NiagaraComponent, CustomParameters);
}
```

Now, when you call `MyExampleFunction()`, it will create a `UNiagaraComponent` with the Niagara System assigned from the Blueprint, attach it to the root component of the actor, and set the custom user parameters.

I apologize for the confusion. The `SetMyCustomFloatParameter` function is a custom function that was defined in the previous explanations. It is not part of Unreal Engine's API.

To clarify, `SetMyCustomFloatParameter` is a custom function within a custom function library named `UMyNiagaraFunctionLibrary`. You need to create this library yourself using the provided code samples in the previous explanations. Once the custom function library is created, you can use the custom `SetMyCustomFloatParameter` function to set your user parameters in your Niagara component.

Here are the header and implementation files for creating the custom Niagara Function Library:

**MyNiagaraFunctionLibrary.h:**
```cpp
// MyNiagaraFunctionLibrary.h

#pragma once
#include "CoreMinimal.h"
#include "MyNiagaraCustomParameters.h" 
#include "Kismet/BlueprintFunctionLibrary.h"
#include "NiagaraFunctionLibrary.h"
#include "MyNiagaraFunctionLibrary.generated.h"

UCLASS()
class UMyNiagaraFunctionLibrary : public UNiagaraFunctionLibrary
{
    GENERATED_BODY()

public:
    // Declare your custom functions here
    UFUNCTION(BlueprintCallable, Category = "My Niagara Functions")
    static void SetMyCustomFloatParameter(UNiagaraComponent* NiagaraComponent, FMyNiagaraCustomParameters CustomParameters);
};
```

**MyNiagaraFunctionLibrary.cpp:**
```cpp
// MyNiagaraFunctionLibrary.cpp

#include "MyNiagaraFunctionLibrary.h"
#include "NiagaraComponent.h"
#include "NiagaraDataInterface.h"
#include "NiagaraTypes.h"

void UMyNiagaraFunctionLibrary::SetMyCustomFloatParameter(UNiagaraComponent* NiagaraComponent, FMyNiagaraCustomParameters CustomParameters)
{
    if (NiagaraComponent)
    {
        // Get the UserRedirectionParameterStore from the Niagara component
        FNiagaraUserRedirectionParameterStore& UserParameterStore = NiagaraComponent->GetUserRedirectionParameterStore();

        // Assign your custom parameters to the corresponding Niagara variables
        UserParameterStore.SetParameterValue(CustomParameters.MyCustomFloat, CustomParameters.Parameter, false);

        // Ensure the component is updated with the new parameter values
        NiagaraComponent->ReinitializeSystem();
    }
}
```

Once you have created the custom function library with the provided code samples, you will have a method named `SetMyCustomFloatParameter` as part of your own custom library for setting user parameters in Niagara components.

To terminate the `UNiagaraComponent` after using it once or after a single loop of the particle system, you can set the `bAutoDestroy` property of the component to `true` and ensure the Niagara System has `System Lifecycle Mode` set to `Complete`.

Here's a step-by-step process:

1. In the C++ code where you create and initialize the `UNiagaraComponent`, set `bAutoDestroy` to `true`:

```cpp
UNiagaraComponent* NiagaraComponent = NewObject<UNiagaraComponent>(this);
NiagaraComponent->SetAsset(NiagaraSystemReference);
NiagaraComponent->SetAutoDestroy(true); // Set AutoDestroy
NiagaraComponent->RegisterComponentWithWorld(GetWorld());
NiagaraComponent->AttachToComponent(GetRootComponent(), FAttachmentTransformRules::KeepRelativeTransform);
NiagaraComponent->ActivateSystem();
```

2. Open your `UNiagaraSystem` in the Niagara Editor. In the Emitter tab, click on the emitter and go to the `Emitter Update` script. In the script, you should see a "Complete" mode node, which indicates that the emitter ends after a single loop.

3. Go to the System tab and click on your `UNiagaraSystem`. In the Details panel, find the `System Lifecycle Mode` property and set it to `Complete`. This ensures that the system terminates after playing the emitter(s) once.

Now, when the particle system plays once, the `UNiagaraComponent` will automatically be destroyed because of the `bAutoDestroy` property. The Niagara System and emitter(s) are configured to terminate after a single loop, which prevents it from looping indefinitely.

Remember to save your Niagara System and re-compile it before testing this in your game.

