Creating a full racing game requires a lot of code and is beyond the scope of this platform. However, I can provide you with a simple text-based racing game using the C++ programming language as a starting point for you to expand upon. You may want to research game development frameworks like Unity or Unreal Engine if you want to make an advanced racing game with graphics.

Here's a simple text-based racing game in C++:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <ctime>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <unistd.h>

const int trackWidth = 20;
const int trackLength = 40;

void drawTrack(const char track[][trackLength], int playerPosition) {
    for (int i = 0; i < trackWidth; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j < trackLength; ++j) {
            if (i == playerPosition && j == trackLength / 2) {
                std::cout << 'P';
            } else {
                std::cout << track[i][j];
            }
        }
        std::cout << "\n";
    }
}

void movePlayer(int &playerPosition, char direction) {
    if (direction == 'a' && playerPosition > 0) {
        --playerPosition;
    } else if (direction == 'd' && playerPosition < trackWidth - 1) {
        ++playerPosition;
    }
}

int main() {
    srand(time(0));

    char track[trackWidth][trackLength] = {};

    for (int i = 0; i < trackWidth; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j < trackLength; ++j) {
            if (j == 0 || j == trackLength - 1) {
                track[i][j] = '|';
            } else {
                track[i][j] = ' ';
            }
        }
    }

    int playerPosition = trackWidth / 2;
    int laps = 3;
    int lapDistance = 0;

    while (laps > 0) {
        system("clear"); // For Windows use: system("cls");

        drawTrack(track, playerPosition);

        char input;
        std::cin >> input;
        movePlayer(playerPosition, input);

        int move = rand() % 3 - 1;
        if (playerPosition + move >= 0 && playerPosition + move < trackWidth) {
            playerPosition += move;
        }

        if (++lapDistance >= trackLength) {
            --laps;
            lapDistance = 0;
        }

        usleep(50000); // Sleep for 50 milliseconds
    }

    std::cout << "Congratulations! You finished the race!\n";

    return 0;
}
```

This code is a basic representation of a racing game where you control a car (P) between the tracks, and you can move left (a) and right (d) on the track. The car moves forward automatically, and you have to complete `laps` number of laps (default: 3) to finish the game.

Here's a simple jumping game using JavaScript and HTML5 Canvas. Create an `index.html` file and add the following code:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Jumping Game</title>
    <style>
        canvas {
            display: block;
            margin: auto;
            background-color: #f0f0f0;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <canvas id="gameCanvas" width="640" height="360"></canvas>
    <script>
        const canvas = document.getElementById("gameCanvas");
        const ctx = canvas.getContext("2d");

        const gravity = 0.5;
        const groundHeight = 40;

        let isJumping = false;
        let jumpHeight = 0;
        let velocity = 0;

        const character = {
            x: 100,
            y: canvas.height - groundHeight - 50,
            width: 50,
            height: 50,
        };

        function startJump() {
            if (!isJumping) {
                isJumping = true;
                velocity = -15;
            }
        }

        function update() {
            if (isJumping) {
                character.y += velocity;
                velocity += gravity;
                if (character.y >= canvas.height - groundHeight - character.height) {
                    character.y = canvas.height - groundHeight - character.height;
                    isJumping = false;
                }
            }
            draw();
        }

        function draw() {
            // Clear canvas
            ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);

            // Draw character
            ctx.fillStyle = "#000";
            ctx.fillRect(character.x, character.y, character.width, character.height);

            // Draw ground
            ctx.fillStyle = "#444";
            ctx.fillRect(0, canvas.height - groundHeight, canvas.width, groundHeight);
        }

        // Mouse event to trigger jump
        canvas.addEventListener("mousedown", startJump);

        // Keyboard event `space` to trigger jump
        document.addEventListener("keydown", (event) => {
            if (event.code === "Space") {
                event.preventDefault(); // prevent scrolling the web page
                startJump();
            }
        });

        setInterval(update, 1000/60); // 60 FPS
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

In this game, we have a simple character represented by a square that can jump when you click the canvas or press the space key. The character is affected by gravity, and there's a ground to indicate when the jump is over.

To run the game, simply open the `index.html` file in your web browser.

