To convert an array to a set in C++, you can use the following code:

```c++
#include <set>

// Declare array
int arr[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

// Get size of array
int size = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);

// Convert array to set
std::set<int> myset(arr, arr + size);
```

This code first declares an array called `arr` with some values. It then calculates the size of the array by dividing the total size of the array by the size of each element.

Finally, it converts the array to a set using the constructor of the `std::set` class that takes two iterators: the first iterator points to the beginning of the array (`arr`), and the second iterator points to the end of the array (`arr + size`). This constructor copies the elements in the array to the set.

After this code is executed, `myset` will contain the same elements as `arr`, but in sorted order and with no duplicates.

To convert a 2D array into an array of sets in C++, you can use the following code:

```c++
#include <set>

// Declare 2D array
int arr[][3] = {{1,2,3}, {4,5,6}, {7,8,9}};

// Get number of rows and columns
int rows = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
int cols = sizeof(arr[0]) / sizeof(arr[0][0]);

// Convert 2D array to array of sets
std::set<int> myset[cols];
for (int c = 0; c < cols; ++c) {
    for (int r = 0; r < rows; ++r) {
        myset[c].insert(arr[r][c]);
    }
}
```

This code first declares a 2D array called `arr`. It then calculates the number of rows and columns by dividing the total size of the array by the size of the first element (i.e., one row).

Finally, it converts the 2D array to an array of sets using a nested loop. The outer loop iterates over the columns of the array, and for each column creates an empty set in the `myset` array. The inner loop iterates over the rows of the array and inserts each element of the column into the corresponding set in `myset`.

After this code is executed, `myset` will be an array of sets where each set contains the unique elements of the corresponding column of `arr`.

To declare a 2D array of sets in C++, you can use the following code:

```c++
#include <set>

// Declare 2D array of sets
std::set<int> arr[3][3];
```

This code declares a 2D array of sets called `arr` with dimensions 3x3. Each element of `arr` is a set of integers.

You can then use this array like a normal 2D array, but with sets instead of individual elements. For example, you can insert an element into the set at position (i, j) using the `insert` method:

```c++
arr[i][j].insert(42);
```

Or you can check if a set contains an element using the `count` method:

```c++
if (arr[i][j].count(42)) {
    // The set at position (i, j) contains 42
}
```

