Building a complete drum sequencer requires a significant amount of code, so I'll give you a basic structure and a simple example to get you started. You can expand upon this example to create a more feature-rich sequencer as needed.

To build a drum sequencer in JavaScript, you'll need to utilize the Web Audio API to handle audio playback, and HTML/CSS/JavaScript will be used for the sequencer's grid and user interaction.

Here's a simple example of a drum sequencer with one sound and four steps:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Drum Sequencer</title>
    <style>
        .grid {
            display: flex;
        }
        .cell {
            width: 50px;
            height: 50px;
            border: 1px solid #ccc;
            text-align: center;
            line-height: 50px;
            user-select: none;
        }
        .active {
            background-color: #333;
            color: #fff;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="grid" id="grid">
        <div class="cell">1</div>
        <div class="cell">2</div>
        <div class="cell">3</div>
        <div class="cell">4</div>
    </div>

    <button onclick="startSequence()">Start</button>
    <button onclick="stopSequence()">Stop</button>

    <script>
        const grid = document.getElementById('grid');
        const cells = Array.from(grid.getElementsByClassName('cell'));
        let currentStep = 0;
        let interval;

        grid.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
            if (e.target.classList.contains('cell')) {
                e.target.classList.toggle('active');
            }
        });

        function startSequence() {
            if (interval) return;
            interval = setInterval(() => {
                if (cells[currentStep].classList.contains('active')) {
                    playSound();
                }
                cells[currentStep].style.backgroundColor = 'red';
                setTimeout(() => {
                    cells[currentStep].style.backgroundColor = cells[currentStep].classList.contains('active') ? '#333' : '';
                }, 100);
                currentStep = (currentStep + 1) % cells.length;
            }, 500);
        }

        function stopSequence() {
            clearInterval(interval);
            interval = null;
        }

        async function playSound() {
            const audioContext = new AudioContext();
            const response = await fetch('https://www.soundjay.com/misc/sounds/bell-ringing-01.mp3');
            const audioData = await response.arrayBuffer();
            const buffer = await audioContext.decodeAudioData(audioData);
            const source = audioContext.createBufferSource();
            source.buffer = buffer;
            source.connect(audioContext.destination);
            source.start(0);
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

In this example, a simple grid is created using HTML and CSS, with JavaScript handling user interaction and audio. When a user clicks on a cell, it becomes active, and when the sequencer is started, each cell will be played sequentially. The playback will loop until the sequencer is stopped.

The provided code loads an external sound file and uses the Web Audio API to play the sound. Please make sure to test this code on a browser that supports Web Audio API.

This is just a very basic example. A complete drum sequencer would typically include multiple tracks for different drum sounds, adjustable tempo, save/load functionality, and possibly a more advanced user interface for editing beats.

Feel free to improve and expand upon this example to create the exact drum sequencer you have in mind!

