Step 1: Set up your Spring Boot project
Step 2: Add required dependencies
Step 3: Configure application properties
Step 4: Enable OAuth2 and OIDC in the Security configuration
Step 5: Create a custom authentication success handler
Step 6: Implement a user details service
Step 7: Test your implementation

Now, let's discuss each step in detail.

Step 1: Set up your Spring Boot project
To get started, create a new Spring Boot project using either Spring Initializr (https://start.spring.io/) or your favorite IDE. Generate the project with the default options selected.

Step 2: Add required dependencies
Add the following dependencies to your `pom.xml` (Maven) or `build.gradle` (Gradle):
- Spring Security: Required for the security configuration.
- Spring Security OAuth2 Client: Required for OAuth2 client features.
- Spring Security OAuth2 JOSE: Required for JWT support.

For Maven, add these dependencies:

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.security</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-security-oauth2-client</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.security</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-security-oauth2-jose</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

For Gradle, add:

```groovy
implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-security'
implementation 'org.springframework.security:spring-security-oauth2-client'
implementation 'org.springframework.security:spring-security-oauth2-jose'
```

Step 3: Configure application properties
Update your `application.properties` or `application.yml` file with the necessary OAuth2 and OIDC settings, such as the client ID, client secret, issuer URI, and other details required by your identity provider.

For example:

```
spring.security.oauth2.client.registration.google.client-id=<your-client-id>
spring.security.oauth2.client.registration.google.client-secret=<your-client-secret>
spring.security.oauth2.client.registration.google.scope=openid,profile,email
spring.security.oauth2.client.registration.google.redirect-uri-template={baseUrl}/login/oauth2/code/{registrationId}

spring.security.oauth2.client.provider.google.issuer-uri=https://accounts.google.com
```

Step 4: Enable OAuth2 and OIDC in the Security configuration
Create a configuration class that extends `WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter` and overrides the `configure` method. Enable the OAuth2 login and configure the authentication success handler.

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SecurityConfig extends WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter {

    @Autowired
    private CustomAuthenticationSuccessHandler successHandler;

    @Override
    protected void configure(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .authorizeRequests()
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
                .and()
            .oauth2Login()
                .successHandler(successHandler);
    }
}
```

Step 5: Create a custom authentication success handler
Develop a custom success handler that implements `AuthenticationSuccessHandler` and does any extra processing needed upon successful authentication, such as fetching user details or syncing them with your database.

```java
@Component
public class CustomAuthenticationSuccessHandler implements AuthenticationSuccessHandler {

    @Autowired
    private CustomUserDetailsService userDetailsService;

    @Override
    public void onAuthenticationSuccess(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response,
        Authentication authentication) throws IOException, ServletException {

        OAuth2AuthenticationToken token = (OAuth2AuthenticationToken) authentication;

        // Fetch user details
        CustomUserDetails userDetails = userDetailsService.getUserDetails(token);

        // Do any extra work needed, such as saving user details to your database

        // Redirect to the default target URL after successful authentication
        response.sendRedirect("/default");
    }
}
```

Step 6: Implement a user details service
Create a service that implements `CustomUserDetails` and interacts with the `OAuth2AuthenticationToken` object to fetch user details from the identity provider.

```java
@Service
public class CustomUserDetailsService {

    public CustomUserDetails getUserDetails(OAuth2AuthenticationToken token) {
        CustomUserDetails userDetails = new CustomUserDetails();

        // Fetch user details from the token
        // You can also use the `DefaultOAuth2UserService` or `OidcUserService` to load user details automatically
        userDetails.setEmail(token.getPrincipal().getAttribute("email"));
        userDetails.setName(token.getPrincipal().getAttribute("name"));

        return userDetails;
    }
}
```

Step 7: Test your implementation
Run your Spring Boot application, navigate to the protected resources, and verify that the OAuth2 and OIDC authentication works as expected. Upon successful authentication, you should be redirected to the target URL specified in your custom success handler.

Step 1: Set up your Spring Boot project with security dependencies
Step 2: Create the User, Role, and Permission entities
Step 3: Implement a user details service
Step 4: Enable global method security
Step 5: Implement a security configuration
Step 6: Define roles and permissions for your application
Step 7: Secure your application using role-based access control

Now, let's discuss each step in detail.

Step 1: Set up your Spring Boot project with security dependencies
Create a new Spring Boot project using Spring Initializr (https://start.spring.io/) or your favorite IDE, and include the Spring Security (Spring Boot Starter Security) dependency.

Step 2: Create the User, Role, and Permission entities
Develop your User, Role, and Permission entities according to your application's requirements, and establish their relationships. For example:

```java
@Entity
public class User {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.AUTO)
    private Long id;
    private String username;

    @ManyToMany(fetch = FetchType.EAGER)
    private Set<Role> roles;
}

@Entity
public class Role {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.AUTO)
    private Long id;
    private String name;

    @ManyToMany(fetch = FetchType.EAGER)
    private Set<Permission> permissions;
}

@Entity
public class Permission {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.AUTO)
    private Long id;
    private String name;
}
```

Step 3: Implement a user details service
Create a class that implements the `UserDetailsService` interface and is responsible for fetching user details and authorities (roles and permissions) from the database.

```java
@Service
public class CustomUserDetailsService implements UserDetailsService {

    @Autowired
    private UserRepository userRepository;

    @Override
    public UserDetails loadUserByUsername(String username) throws UsernameNotFoundException {
        User user = userRepository.findByUsername(username);
        if (user == null) {
            throw new UsernameNotFoundException("User not found");
        }

        return new org.springframework.security.core.userdetails.User(
            user.getUsername(),
            user.getPassword(),
            getAuthorities(user.getRoles())
        );
    }

    private Collection<? extends GrantedAuthority> getAuthorities(Set<Role> roles) {
        return roles.stream()
            .flatMap(role -> role.getPermissions().stream())
            .map(permission -> new SimpleGrantedAuthority(permission.getName()))
            .collect(Collectors.toSet());
    }
}
```

Step 4: Enable global method security
Update your `SecurityConfig` class and add `@EnableGlobalMethodSecurity` with the desired `securedEnabled`, `prePostEnabled`, or `jsr250Enabled` settings.

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
@EnableGlobalMethodSecurity(securedEnabled = true, prePostEnabled = true, jsr250Enabled = true)
public class SecurityConfig extends WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter {
    // Security configuration
}
```

Step 5: Implement a security configuration
Create a configuration class that extends `WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter` and implements the desired authorization rules:

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
@EnableGlobalMethodSecurity(prePostEnabled = true)
public class SecurityConfig extends WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter {

    @Autowired
    private CustomUserDetailsService userDetailsService;

    @Override
    protected void configure(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .authorizeRequests()
                .antMatchers("/public").permitAll()
                .antMatchers("/admin").hasRole("ADMIN")
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
                .and()
            .formLogin()
                .and()
            .httpBasic()
                .and()
            .csrf().disable();
    }

    @Override
    protected void configure(AuthenticationManagerBuilder auth) throws Exception {
        auth.userDetailsService(userDetailsService).passwordEncoder(passwordEncoder());
    }

    @Bean
    public PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {
        return new BCryptPasswordEncoder();
    }
}
```

Step 6: Define roles and permissions for your application
Create roles and permissions for your application and store them in the database, for example: `ROLE_ADMIN`, `ROLE_USER`, etc. Assign the appropriate permissions to each role.

Step 7: Secure your application using role-based access control
After enabling global method security, you can use the `@Secured`, `@PreAuthorize`, or `@RolesAllowed` annotations to secure your application, for example:

```java
@RestController
public class SampleController {

    @GetMapping("/admin")
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
    public String admin() {
        return "Admin content";
    }

    @GetMapping("/user")
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('USER')")
    public String user() {
        return "User content";
    }
}
```

Now your application should have role-based access control, and only users with the required roles will be able to access certain protected resources.

