To demonstrate server-side rendering for showing flight information, I will use Node.js with Express as the server framework, Socket.IO for real-time data transfer, and Handlebars as the template engine. We'll store flight information in a simple in-memory data store, but you can easily adapt it to use a real database.

1. Install the required packages:

```
npm install express socket.io express-handlebars
```

2. Now let's create `app.js`:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const http = require('http');
const socketIo = require('socket.io');
const exphbs = require('express-handlebars');

const app = express();
const server = http.createServer(app);
const io = socketIo(server);

// Set up Handlebars as the template engine
app.engine('handlebars', exphbs());
app.set('view engine', 'handlebars');

// In-memory data store for flight information
const flightData = {};

// Function to update flight data
function updateFlightData(flight, data) {
  flightData[flight] = data;
  // Emit an event to all connected clients with the flight data
  io.emit('flightData', { flight, data });
}

// Serve the flight data entry page
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.render('dataEntry', { flightData });
});

// Serve the flight data display page
app.get('/display', (req, res) => {
  res.render('flightDisplay', { flightData });
});

// Set up Socket.IO connection
io.on('connection', (socket) => {
  console.log('A user connected');

  // Listen for flight data updates from clients
  socket.on('updateFlightData', (data) => {
    updateFlightData(data.flight, data.data);
  });

  socket.on('disconnect', () => {
    console.log('A user disconnected');
  });
});

// Start the server
const port = 3000;
server.listen(port, () => {
  console.log(`Server running at http://localhost:${port}`);
});
```

In this file, we have set up a basic Express application with Socket.IO for real-time updates. We have two routes, `/` and `/display`, which render different views for entering and displaying flight data. When a connected client sends a `updateFlightData` event, the server updates the data in memory and broadcasts the updates to all other connected clients.

3. Create a Handlebars template for the data entry page. Create a file called `views/dataEntry.handlebars`:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Flight Data Entry</title>
</head>
<body>
  <form id="data-entry-form">
    Flight number: <input type="text" id="flight-input" />
    Flight data: <input type="text" id="data-input" />
    <button type="submit">Submit</button>
  </form>
<script src="/socket.io/socket.io.js"></script>
<script>
const socket = io();

document.getElementById('data-entry-form').addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();
  const flight = document.getElementById('flight-input').value;
  const data = document.getElementById('data-input').value;

  socket.emit('updateFlightData', { flight, data });
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

This template renders a simple form where users can enter flight data. When the form is submitted, we emit a `updateFlightData` event to the server with the flight data.

4. Create a Handlebars template for the flight data display page, called `views/flightDisplay.handlebars`:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Flight Data Display</title>
</head>
<body>
  <ul id="flight-display">
    {{#each flightData}}
      <li id="flight-{{@key}}">{{@key}}: {{this}}</li>
    {{/each}}
  </ul>
<script src="/socket.io/socket.io.js"></script>
<script>
const socket = io();

socket.on('flightData', (data) => {
  const flightDisplay = document.getElementById('flight-display');
  const flightItem = document.getElementById(`flight-${data.flight}`);

  if (flightItem) {
    flightItem.innerText = `${data.flight}: ${data.data}`;
  } else {
    const newFlightItem = document.createElement('li');
    newFlightItem.id = `flight-${data.flight}`;
    newFlightItem.innerText = `${data.flight}: ${data.data}`;
    flightDisplay.appendChild(newFlightItem);
  }
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

This template renders a list of flight information. When a `flightData` event is received from the server, we update the corresponding list item or create a new one if necessary.

5. Now start the server:

```
node app.js
```

With this code, open two browser windows: one at `http://localhost:3000/` and another at `http://localhost:3000/display`. If you enter flight data in the first window, you should see the flight data get updated in real-time in the second window.

