Sure, here's an implementation of the QuickSort algorithm in C# with explanations:

```csharp
public static void QuickSort(int[] arr, int left, int right)
{
    // Check if there are two or more elements in the array to sort
    if (left < right)
    {
        // Partition the array into two parts
        int pivotIndex = Partition(arr, left, right);

        // Recursively sort the two parts using QuickSort
        QuickSort(arr, left, pivotIndex - 1);
        QuickSort(arr, pivotIndex + 1, right);
    }
}

private static int Partition(int[] arr, int left, int right)
{
    // Choose the last element in the array as the pivot element
    int pivot = arr[right];

    // Set the pivot index to the leftmost position
    int pivotIndex = left;

    // Iterate through the array from left to right
    for (int i = left; i < right; i++)
    {
        // If the current element is smaller than or equal to the pivot element, swap it with the element at the pivot index
        if (arr[i] <= pivot)
        {
            Swap(arr, i, pivotIndex);
            pivotIndex++;
        }
    }

    // Swap the pivot element with the element at the pivot index
    Swap(arr, pivotIndex, right);

    // Return the index of the pivot element
    return pivotIndex;
}

private static void Swap(int[] arr, int i, int j)
{
    int temp = arr[i];
    arr[i] = arr[j];
    arr[j] = temp;
}
```

Explanation of the algorithm:

1. The `QuickSort` method is called with the array to sort, the leftmost index, and the rightmost index as parameters. If there are less than 2 elements to sort (i.e., `left >= right`), the method does nothing and returns.
2. The `Partition` method is called to partition the array into two parts: one with elements smaller than the pivot element located at its left side, and the other part with elements larger than the pivot element located at its right side. The pivot element is chosen as the last element of the array.
3. The two parts of the array are recursively sorted using the same process:
   - If the leftmost index is smaller than the pivot index minus one, a recursive call to `QuickSort` is made for the left part of the array.
   - If the rightmost index is greater than the pivot index plus one, a recursive call to `QuickSort` is made for the right part of the array.
4. The `Partition` method moves all elements less than the pivot element to the left side, and all elements greater than the pivot element to the right side. It returns the index of the pivot element.
5. The `Swap` method is a helper function to swap two elements in the array.

