```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <math.h> // for sine and cosine

#define PI 3.14159265358979323846

// define complex number structure
typedef struct {
    double real;
    double imag;
} complex;

// function to perform FFT
void FFT(complex *x, int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return; // base case
    complex even[n/2], odd[n/2];
    for (int i = 0; i < n/2; i++) {
        even[i] = x[2*i];
        odd[i] = x[2*i+1];
    }
    FFT(even, n/2); // recursive call for even part
    FFT(odd, n/2);  // recursive call for odd part
    for (int i = 0; i < n/2; i++) {
        complex t = {cos(2 * PI * i / n), -sin(2 * PI * i / n)}; // twiddle factor
        t.real *= odd[i].real;
        t.imag *= odd[i].imag;
        x[i] = (complex) {even[i].real + t.real, even[i].imag + t.imag};  // update first half of array
        x[i + n/2] = (complex) {even[i].real - t.real, even[i].imag - t.imag}; // update second half of array
    }
}

int main() {
    // generate random input data
    int n = 8;
    double x[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8};
    complex data[n];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        data[i] = (complex) {x[i], 0}; // convert input data to complex numbers
    }

    // perform FFT
    FFT(data, n);

    // print results
    printf("FFT output:\n");
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("%g + %g i\n", data[i].real, data[i].imag);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this code example, we first define a complex number struct that contains a real and imaginary part. We then define a recursive FFT function that uses the twiddle factor formula to calculate the FFT of an input array of complex numbers. Next, we generate some random input data `x` and convert it to an array of complex numbers. We then call the FFT function on the input data and print the results.

Note that this code example assumes that the input data size is a power of 2. In practice, you will want to handle input data sizes that are not power of 2 by padding the input data with zeros. Additionally, this code example does not take into account any windowing functions that are generally used before performing the FFT.

