In Puppeteer, `page.evaluate` and `page.$$eval` are used to run JavaScript code within the context of the page. `page.evaluate` is used to execute a single JavaScript expression or a function, while `page.$$eval` is useful for looping over elements that match a CSS selector.

Here is a practical example:

1. `page.evaluate()`: Get the inner text of the first element with a specific class

```javascript
const puppeteer = require('puppeteer');

(async () => {
  const browser = await puppeteer.launch();
  const page = await browser.newPage();
  await page.goto('https://example.com'); // Replace with the URL you want to scrape

  const elementText = await page.evaluate(() => {
    const element = document.querySelector('.example-class');
    return element ? element.innerText : null;
  });

  console.log('Element text:', elementText);

  await browser.close();
})();
```

In this example, we use `page.evaluate` to run a function that gets the first element with the class "example-class" and returns the inner text of that element.

2. `page.$$eval()`: Get the inner text of all elements with a specific class

```javascript
const puppeteer = require('puppeteer');

(async () => {
  const browser = await puppeteer.launch();
  const page = await browser.newPage();
  await page.goto('https://example.com'); // Replace with the URL you want to scrape

  const elementsText = await page.$$eval('.example-class', elements =>
    elements.map(element => element.innerText)
  );

  console.log('Elements text:', elementsText);

  await browser.close();
})();
```

In this example, we use `page.$$eval` to pass a function that maps all elements with the class "example-class" to their inner text. It returns an array of strings, representing the inner text of each element.

Both `page.evaluate()` and `page.$$eval()` are powerful methods for interacting with the DOM and extracting information from web pages using Puppeteer.

