In order to access window variables in React, you can use the global window object, which is accessible from anywhere in your application.

Here's an example of how to access a window variable in React:

```jsx
import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

function MyComponent() {
  const [myVariable, setMyVariable] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    // Access window variable and update state
    setMyVariable(window.myVariable);
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Window Variable: {myVariable}</p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

In this example, we use the `useEffect` hook to access the window variable and update the component's state with the value of the variable. We do this only once, when the component mounts, by passing an empty array as the dependency array to the `useEffect` hook.

Note that accessing window variables directly can be risky and may introduce potential security vulnerabilities to your application. It's generally recommended to use a more secure approach such as passing the variable to React as a prop instead of accessing it directly.

