    std::vector<int> chain = simulateMarkovChain(transition_matrix, steps, initial_state);

    // Print the generated Markov chain
    std::cout << "Generated Markov chain: " << std::endl;
    for (int state : chain) {
        std::cout << state << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, I implemented an S-order Markov chain for numerical sequences, where S=1 (first-order), with a simple transition matrix representing the transition probabilities between two numerical states 0 and 1. The chain starts with an initial state (0 in this case), and the `simulateMarkovChain` function generates a chain of the specified length (`steps`) using the given transition matrix.

You can adapt this code to implement an S-order Markov chain by modifying the transition matrix to work with S-length combinations of states and providing an initial state of the same length. Remember, the higher the order of the chain, the larger the size of the transition matrix will be.

