To send a message and receive a BasicNack in C# using the RabbitMQ.Client library, follow these steps:

1. Install the RabbitMQ.Client NuGet package to your project.

2. Import the necessary namespaces:

```csharp
using System.Text;
using RabbitMQ.Client;
using RabbitMQ.Client.Events;
```

3. Create a connection and a model (channel) to communicate with RabbitMQ:

```csharp
ConnectionFactory factory = new ConnectionFactory() { HostName = "localhost" };
using IConnection connection = factory.CreateConnection();
using IModel channel = connection.CreateModel();
```

4. Enable publisher confirms for the channel:

```csharp
channel.ConfirmSelect();
```

5. Declare the exchange and queue:

```csharp
channel.ExchangeDeclare("exchange_name", "direct");
channel.QueueDeclare("queue_name", durable: true, exclusive: false, autoDelete: false);
channel.QueueBind("queue_name", "exchange_name", "routing_key");
```

6. Handle the BasicNack and BasicAck events:

```csharp
channel.BasicNacks += (sender, args) =>
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Message Nacked, deliveryTag: {args.DeliveryTag}, multiple: {args.Multiple}");
};

channel.BasicAcks += (sender, args) =>
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Message Acked, deliveryTag: {args.DeliveryTag}, multiple: {args.Multiple}");
};
```

7. Send a message:

```csharp
IBasicProperties properties = channel.CreateBasicProperties();
properties.Persistent = true;

byte[] body = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("Hello, nack this message!");

channel.BasicPublish("exchange_name", "routing_key", mandatory: false, properties, body: body);
```

8. Set up a consumer to consume and reject (BasicNack) the message:

```csharp
var consumer = new EventingBasicConsumer(channel);

consumer.Received += (model, ea) =>
{
    var body = ea.Body.ToArray();
    var message = Encoding.UTF8.GetString(body);

    Console.WriteLine("Message received: {0}", message);

    // Reject the message with BasicNack (requeue: false, multiple: false)
    channel.BasicNack(ea.DeliveryTag, multiple: false, requeue: false);
};

channel.BasicConsume(queue: "queue_name", autoAck: false, consumer: consumer);
```

9. Finally, if you want to wait for publisher confirms (BasicAck):

```csharp
channel.WaitForConfirmsOrDie(new TimeSpan(0, 0, 5)); // 5 seconds timeout
```

Now, when you run this code, it will send a message and receive a BasicNack from the consumer.

*Note: The example shown assumes that you have a running RabbitMQ server and the library `RabbitMQ.Client` installed.

One option would be to create a custom ItemReader that retrieves the data from your HashMap and passes it to the ItemProcessor. You can then inject this custom ItemReader into your Spring Batch job configuration. 

Here's an example implementation:

```java
public class HashMapItemReader implements ItemReader<Map<String, Object>> {

    private Map<String, Object> data;

    public HashMapItemReader(Map<String, Object> data) {
        this.data = data;
    }

    @Override
    public Map<String, Object> read() throws Exception {
        return data;
    }

}
```

You can then inject this custom ItemReader into your job configuration:

```java
@Bean
public Job myJob(JobBuilderFactory jobBuilderFactory, StepBuilderFactory stepBuilderFactory, ItemProcessor<Map<String, Object>, Map<String, Object>> itemProcessor, JobCompletionNotificationListener listener) {
    return jobBuilderFactory.get("myJob")
            .incrementer(new RunIdIncrementer())
            .listener(listener)
            .start(stepBuilderFactory.get("step1")
                    .<Map<String, Object>, Map<String, Object>>chunk(10)
                    .reader(new HashMapItemReader(myData))
                    .processor(itemProcessor)
                    .writer(new ConsoleItemWriter<>())
                    .build())
            .build();
}
```

Here, `myData` is your HashMap that you want to pass to the ItemProcessor. It is passed to the constructor of the custom ItemReader, which simply returns the whole HashMap as the read item. The main job configuration injects this custom ItemReader into the step's reader method, along with the ItemProcessor and other required configurations.

One approach you could take would be to use a custom JobParameter that holds your map data. You can then inject this custom JobParameter into your ItemProcessor as a method parameter. 

First, you'll need to create a custom JobParameter that holds your map data:

```java
public class MapJobParameter implements JobParameter {

    private Map<String, Object> map;

    public MapJobParameter(Map<String, Object> map) {
        this.map = map;
    }

    @Override
    public Object getValue() {
        return map;
    }

    // You can leave the following methods as is, or provide your own implementations:

    @Override
    public String getType() {
        return "map";
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return map.toString();
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return map.hashCode();
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        if (this == obj) {
            return true;
        }
        if (obj == null) {
            return false;
        }
        if (getClass() != obj.getClass()) {
            return false;
        }
        MapJobParameter other = (MapJobParameter) obj;
        if (map == null) {
            if (other.map != null) {
                return false;
            }
        } else if (!map.equals(other.map)) {
            return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

}
```

Next, when launching your job, you can create a JobParameters object and set your custom JobParameter as one of the parameters:

```java
Map<String, Object> myData = // your map data here

JobParameters jobParameters = new JobParametersBuilder()
        .addParameter("myData", new MapJobParameter(myData))
        .toJobParameters();

jobLauncher.run(myJob, jobParameters);
```

Finally, in your ItemProcessor implementation, you can add a method parameter that will be automatically injected with the value of your custom JobParameter:

```java
@Component
public class MyItemProcessor implements ItemProcessor<MyData, MyData> {

    public MyData process(MyData data, @Value("#{jobParameters['myData'].value}") Map<String, Object> myData) {
        // use myData here as needed...
        // process your item as usual...
        return processedData;
    }

}
```

Here, the `@Value` annotation is used to inject the value of the "myData" parameter from the job parameters. This value is a MapJobParameter object, so we access the actual map value using the `.value` property. This map can then be used in your ItemProcessor to process your items as needed.

Another approach you could take would be to use a Singleton bean to hold your data, which can then be injected into your ItemProcessor. Here's how you could do it:

1. Create a Singleton bean to hold your data:
```java
@Component
@Scope(value="singleton")
public class DataHolder {
    private Map<String, Object> data;

    public Map<String, Object> getData() {
        return data;
    }

    public void setData(Map<String, Object> data) {
        this.data = data;
    }
}
```

2. Populate the data using your service before launching your job:
```java
Map<String, Object> myData = // your map data here
dataHolder.setData(myData); // Inject DataHolder bean into your service class and set data using this method
```

3. Inject the DataHolder bean into your ItemProcessor:
```java
@Component
public class MyItemProcessor implements ItemProcessor<MyData, MyData> {

    @Autowired
    private DataHolder dataHolder;

    public MyData process(MyData data) {
        Map<String, Object> myData = dataHolder.getData(); // Access the data from the DataHolder bean
        // process your item as usual with myData ...
        return processedData;
    }

}
```

By using this approach, you can avoid passing your data as JobParams, but still inject it directly into your ItemProcessor. Just make sure that the DataHolder bean is populated before you launch the job.

