Here's a classical JavaScript solution that finds the biggest consecutive substring where the second one is double the first one:

```javascript
function findBiggestSubstring(s) {
  let biggest = '';
  for (let i = 0; i < s.length - 1; i++) {
    for (let j = i + 1; j < s.length; j++) {
      const sub1 = s.slice(i, j);
      const sub2 = s.slice(j, j + sub1.length);
      if (sub2 === sub1 + sub1 && sub2.length > biggest.length) {
        biggest = sub2;
      }
    }
  }
  return biggest;
}
```

This code uses two nested `for` loops to iterate through all possible pairs of substrings. For each pair, it checks if the second substring is double the first one and if it is bigger than the currently stored `biggest` substring. If it is, it updates the `biggest` variable with the new substring.

Note that this code assumes that the input string is non-empty and only contains lowercase letters (as specified in the original problem statement). If the input can have different constraints, the code may need to be adjusted.

