To convert the initial server program to a secure server program using Java, you can use Java's Secure Socket Extension (JSSE) library, which provides functionalities for secure communication using SSL/TLS. Here are the steps to convert your initial server program:

1. Obtain a certificate: As mentioned earlier, you need to obtain a digital certificate from a Certificate Authority (CA).

2. Create a keystore: Import the certificate into a Java KeyStore (JKS) file. You can use the `keytool` command-line utility included with the JDK (Java Development Kit) to create a keystore and import the certificate. For example:
```
keytool -importcert -file your_certificate.crt -keystore your_keystore.jks -alias server_cert
```
Make sure to replace `your_certificate.crt` with the actual file name of your certificate and `your_keystore.jks` with the desired name for your keystore file.

3. Update the server code to use SSL: Update the server code to use `SSLServerSocket` instead of the standard `ServerSocket`. Here's an example server code excerpt:

```java
import javax.net.ssl.SSLServerSocket;
import javax.net.ssl.SSLServerSocketFactory;
import javax.net.ssl.SSLSocket;
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.io.PrintWriter;
import java.security.Security;

public class SecureServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        System.setProperty("javax.net.ssl.keyStore", "your_keystore.jks");
        System.setProperty("javax.net.ssl.keyStorePassword", "your_keystore_password");

        int port = 8090;

        try {
            SSLServerSocketFactory sslServerSocketFactory = (SSLServerSocketFactory) SSLServerSocketFactory.getDefault();
            SSLServerSocket sslServerSocket = (SSLServerSocket) sslServerSocketFactory.createServerSocket(port);

            System.out.println("Secure server is up and running on port " + port);

            while (true) {
                SSLSocket sslSocket = (SSLSocket) sslServerSocket.accept();

                // Handle client connection using a separate method
                handleClientConnection(sslSocket);
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    private static void handleClientConnection(SSLSocket sslSocket) {
        try {
            BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(sslSocket.getInputStream()));
            PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(sslSocket.getOutputStream(), true);

            String clientRequest = in.readLine();
            // Process clientRequest as needed

            // Send a response message to the client
            out.println("Your Connection request is accepted");

            // Close the connection
            out.close();
            in.close();
            sslSocket.close();

        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
