class Entity {
  constructor(x, y, w, h, speed) {
    this.x = x;
    this.y = y;
    this.w = w;
    this.h = h;
    this.vx = -speed;
  }

  update() {
    this.x += this.vx;
  }

  offScreen(canvas) {
    return this.x < 0;
  }

  collidesWith(obj) {
    return !(obj.y + obj.h <= this.y || obj.y >= this.y + this.h || obj.x + obj.w <= this.x || obj.x >= this.x + this.w);
  }
}

class Projectile {
  constructor(x, y, w, h, angle, speed) {
    this.x = x;
    this.y = y;
    this.w = w;
    this.h = h;
    this.vx = Math.cos(angle) * speed;
    this.vy = Math.sin(angle) * speed;
  }

  update() {
    this.x += this.vx;
    this.y += this.vy;
    this.w *= 0.9;
    this.h *= 0.9;
  }
}

// ...

class Game {
  constructor(canvas) {
    // ...
    this.entities = [];
    this.projectiles = [];
    this.spawnRate = 1; // Spawn rate increases with time
    this.spawnTimer = 0;
    // ...
  }

  updateEntities() {
    this.spawnTimer += 1;
    if (this.spawnTimer > 1 / this.spawnRate) {
      this.spawnTimer = 0;
      this.entities.push(new Entity(canvas.width, Math.random() * canvas.height, 40, 40, 3));
    }

    for (let i = this.entities.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      const entity = this.entities[i];
      entity.update();

      if (entity.offScreen(canvas)) {
        this.entities.splice(i, 1);
      } else if (entity.collidesWith(this.player)) {
        // Entity shoots a projectile
        const angle = Math.atan2(this.player.y - entity.y, this.player.x - entity.x);
        this.projectiles.push(new Projectile(entity.x, entity.y, 10, 10, angle, 5));
      }
    }
  }

  updateProjectiles() {
    for (let i = this.projectiles.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      const projectile = this.projectiles[i];
      projectile.update();

      if (projectile.collidesWith(this.player)) {
        // Apply collision force to player
        const dz = 20; // Change this value to control the collision force
        this.player.vx += projectile.vx / dz;
        this.player.vy += projectile.vy / dz;
      }

      if (projectile.w <= 1) {
        this.projectiles.splice(i, 1);
      }
    }
  }

  update() {
    // ...
    this.updateEntities();
    this.updateProjectiles();
    // ...
    // Draw entities
    this.ctx.fillStyle = "red";
    for (let i = 0; i < this.entities.length; i++) {
      const e = this.entities[i];
      this.ctx.fillRect(e.x, e.y, e.w, e.h);
    }
    // Draw projectiles
    this.ctx.fillStyle = "green";
    for (let i = 0; i < this.projectiles.length; i++) {
      const p = this.projectiles[i];
      this.ctx.fillRect(p.x, p.y, p.w, p.h);
    }
    // ...
  }
}

// ...

let game = new Game(canvas);
```

We've added new classes `Entity` and `Projectile`, and added corresponding arrays and methods to manage them in the `Game` class. Entities spawn from the right side of the screen with a random Y-coordinate, and shoot projectiles towards the player upon collision. Projectiles apply a pushing force to the player upon collision, affecting the player's physics.

To create a gateway application in PHP using the Laravel framework that meets your requirements, follow these steps:

1. Install Laravel
First, install Laravel by following the instructions on the official Laravel installation page: https://laravel.com/docs/8.x/installation

2. Create a new Laravel project
Use the following command to create a new Laravel project:

```
composer create-project laravel/laravel gateway-app
```

Replace "gateway-app" with your desired project name.

3. Set up authentication
To set up authentication using username and password, you can use Laravel's built-in authentication system. Run the following command to generate authentication scaffolding:

```
composer require laravel/ui
php artisan ui bootstrap --auth
```

Now you can customize the 'RegisterController.php' and 'LoginController.php' which are located at 'app/Http/Controllers/Auth' to fit your needs.

4. Create API routes
In 'routes/api.php', create routes for each service/channel that you want to support:

```php
Route::middleware(['auth:api'])->group(function () {
    Route::post('/channel1', 'GatewayController@handleChannel1');
    Route::post('/channel2', 'GatewayController@handleChannel2');
    // Add more routes for each channel
});
```

5. Create a GatewayController
Create a new controller named 'GatewayController.php' in 'app/Http/Controllers':

```php
namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use Illuminate\Http\Request;

class GatewayController extends Controller
{
    public function handleChannel1(Request $request)
    {
        // Handle channel1 requests
    }

    public function handleChannel2(Request $request)
    {
        // Handle channel2 requests
    }

    // Add more methods for each channel
}
```

6. Limit TPS for each channel
To limit the transactions per second (TPS) for each channel, you can either create a custom middleware or use a library like 'GrahamCampbell/Laravel-Throttle'.
To use Laravel-Throttle, install it via composer:

```
composer require graham-campbell/throttle
```

Then add the throttle middleware to the desired routes in 'routes/api.php':

```php
use GrahamCampbell\Throttle\Http\Middleware\ThrottleMiddleware;

Route::middleware(['auth:api', ThrottleMiddleware::class.':60,1'])->group(function () {
    // The limit is per minute, so 60,1 means 60 requests per minute
    Route::post('/channel1', 'GatewayController@handleChannel1');
    Route::post('/channel2', 'GatewayController@handleChannel2');
});
```

7. Validation layer
In each controller method, you can use Laravel's built-in validation to validate the input. For example, in 'handleChannel1' method in 'GatewayController':

```php
public function handleChannel1(Request $request)
{
    $validatedData = $request->validate([
        'field1' => 'required|string',
        'field2' => 'required|integer',
        // Add your validation rules
    ]);

    // Proceed with the request
}
```

8. Transformation layer
For the transformation layer, you can create a separate class or method for each service/channel to convert the input data into the desired format for the top-level application. In this example, I am adding transformation functions directly in the controller:

```php
private function transformChannel1Data(array $data)
{
    // Your transformation logic for channel1
}

private function transformChannel2Data(array $data)
{
    // Your transformation logic for channel2
}
```

Then you can call these transformation functions in their respective controller methods:

```php
public function handleChannel1(Request $request)
{
    $validatedData = $request->validate([
        // Your validation rules
    ]);

    $transformedData = $this->transformChannel1Data($validatedData);

    // Send the request to the top-level application
}

public function handleChannel2(Request $request)
{
    $validatedData = $request->validate([
        // Your validation rules
    ]);

    $transformedData = $this->transformChannel2Data($validatedData);

    // Send the request to the top-level application
}
