To create a Fivem script NUI with an inventory consisting of 6 columns and 5 rows with the specified style, you'll need to use HTML, CSS, and JS for creating the frontend and connecting it to the Fivem backend. Here's a basic structure of the files and code to get you started:

1. Create an HTML file (inventory.html) to structure the inventory:

```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="inventory.css">
    <script src="inventory.js" defer></script>
    <title>Inventory NUI</title>
</head>
<body>

    <div id="inventory">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="item"></div>
            <!-- Repeat the above line 5 more times for a total of 6 items in the row -->
        </div>
        <!-- Repeat the above row code 4 more times for a total of 5 rows -->
    </divider>

</body>
</html>
```

2. Create a CSS file (inventory.css) to style the inventory:

```
body {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}

#inventory {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(6, 1fr);
    grid-template-rows: repeat(5, 1fr);
    grid-gap: 5px;
    width: 600px;
    height: 500px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 10px;
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.7);
    border: 2px solid black;
}

.item {
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.7);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.2);
}
```

3. Create a JS file (inventory.js) to handle events and interaction with the Fivem backend:

```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    // Add your code for handling events and interaction with the Fivem backend here
});
```

4. You'll need to connect the NUI in your Fivem resource by adding it to the resource's __resource.lua or fxmanifest.lua (depending on which you're using).

For __resource.lua:

```lua
ui_page('inventory.html')

files({
    'inventory.html',
    'inventory.css',
    'inventory.js'
})
```

For fxmanifest.lua:

```lua
fx_version 'cerulean'
game 'gta5'

ui_page 'inventory.html'

files {
    'inventory.html',
    'inventory.css',
    'inventory.js'
}
```

Now you have a basic structure for your NUI inventory. From here, you'll need to add your own functionality for handling events, managing inventory data, and interacting with the Fivem backend.

