```java
public static Vector2 velocityToPosition(Body body, Vector2 targetPosition, float maxSpeed) {
    Vector2 currentPosition = body.getPosition();
    Vector2 targetDirection = targetPosition.cpy().sub(currentPosition);

    float distance = targetDirection.len();
    float maxDistancePerFrame = maxSpeed * Gdx.graphics.getDeltaTime();

    // if we can reach the target in one step, go directly to it
    if (distance <= maxDistancePerFrame) {
        return targetDirection;
    }

    Vector2 targetVelocity = targetDirection.nor().scl(maxSpeed);

    // if we would overshoot the target on the next frame, adjust velocity
    if (targetDirection.dot(targetVelocity) * Gdx.graphics.getDeltaTime() > distance) {
        targetVelocity = targetDirection.cpy().setLength2(distance / (2 * Gdx.graphics.getDeltaTime()));
    }

    return targetVelocity.sub(body.getLinearVelocity()).limit(maxSpeed);
}
```
This method takes the current position of the body and the target position, and calculates the direction vector between them. It then checks if the distance to the target is smaller than the maximum distance the body can cover in one frame, and if so, returns the direction vector directly. If not, it sets the target velocity to the maximum speed in the direction vector, and checks if this velocity would overshoot the target on the next frame. If so, it adjusts the velocity to gradually reduce speed as it approaches the target. The method then subtracts the current velocity from the target velocity, limits the resulting vector to the maximum speed, and returns it as the final velocity for the body.

