Here, each route has a view that returns a Promise that resolves to a component. Update the `renderView()` function to handle asynchronous components:

```javascript
async function renderView(routeStack, parent) {
    const { view, params, children } = routeStack.shift() || {};

    if (!view || !parent) {
        return;
    }

    const component = await view(params || {}).then((comp) => comp.default(params));
    parent.innerHTML = '';
    parent.appendChild(component);

    if (routeStack.length > 0) {
        renderView(routeStack, component.querySelector('.child-view'));
    }
}
```

Now, you've advanced the SPA router to:

- Support nested routes via route stack rendering.
- Asynchronously load components, reducing initial loading time and increasing performance.
- Create a container for nested views using `createViewComponent()`.

These enhancements allow you to create a modular, maintainable, and high-performance SPA.

Apologies for the confusion; the `import()` function is not explicitly defined in the previous examples. 

`import()` is a built-in JavaScript feature (Dynamic Imports) that allows you to load modules asynchronously. It returns a Promise that resolves to the module when the module is fetched and executed. You can use dynamic imports to separate parts of your application into separate chunks, reducing the initial loading time and improving performance.

Here's a simple example of how to use `import()` to load a module:

1. Create a file `example-module.js`:

```javascript
// example-module.js
export function greet() {
    console.log('Hello from the example module!');
}
```

2. Use `import()` to dynamically import and use the module in your application:

```javascript
// main.js
document.getElementById('load-module').addEventListener('click', async () => {
    const { greet } = await import('./example-module.js');
    greet();
});
```

3. Create a simple HTML file:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Dynamic Import Example</title>
</head>
<body>
    <button id="load-module">Load Module</button>
    <script src="main.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

Now, when clicking the "Load Module" button, the `example-module.js` will be fetched and executed, displaying "Hello from the example module!" in the console.

