2. Use the Windows Clipboard API functions to read and write to the local clipboard, and to check for changes to the clipboard contents. You can use the SetClipboardViewer function to receive notifications when the contents of the clipboard change.
3. Use a communication protocol (such as TCP/IP) to send the clipboard contents between the local and remote clipboard. You can use Winsock or a network library (such as Boost.Asio) to implement the network communication.
4. In the remote clipboard, use the Clipboard API functions to read and write the clipboard contents received from the local clipboard.
5. To support drag and drop of files between the local and remote clipboard, you can use the Shell API functions to handle file drag and drop events, and to serialize and deserialize file data to send over the network.

