Creating a professional front-end code for a Learning Management System (LMS) requires utilizing various tools and frameworks. One popular front-end framework is React.js, which makes it easy to create interactive components. Here, we will provide you a basic structure and skeleton for a React-based LMS with professional-level front-end code.

1. Set up your environment
Firstly, you will need to install Node.js and then create a new React app using the command:

```
npx create-react-app learning-management-system
cd learning-management-system
```

2. Install necessary dependencies
Install required packages for our LMS (like Bootstrap for styling and React Router for navigating between pages):

```
npm install bootstrap@4.6.0 react-router-dom@5.3.0
```

3. Import dependencies in your `index.js` file:

```javascript
import 'bootstrap/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css';
import { BrowserRouter as Router } from 'react-router-dom';
```

4. Create components structure
For a basic LMS, let's create the following components:
- Header
- Home
- Courses
- Lessons
- Login
- Signup

Create a new folder called `components` inside the `src` folder, and create the components mentioned above inside it:

```
Header.js
Home.js
Courses.js
Lessons.js
Login.js
Signup.js
```

5. Implement your components:

Here's a basic code structure for your `Header.js` component that you can modify to include navigation to other pages:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import { Navbar, Nav } from 'react-bootstrap';
import { Link } from 'react-router-dom';

const Header = () => {
  return (
    <Navbar bg="dark" variant="dark">
      <Navbar.Brand as={Link} to="/">LMS</Navbar.Brand>
      <Nav className="mr-auto">
        <Nav.Link as={Link} to="/courses">Courses</Nav.Link>
        <Nav.Link as={Link} to="/lessons">Lessons</Nav.Link>
        <Nav.Link as={Link} to="/login">Login</Nav.Link>
        <Nav.Link as={Link} to="/signup">Signup</Nav.Link>
      </Nav>
    </Navbar>
  );
};

export default Header;
```

6. Set up routing in your `App.js`:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import { Switch, Route } from 'react-router-dom';
import Header from './components/Header';
import Home from './components/Home';
import Courses from './components/Courses';
import Lessons from './components/Lessons';
import Login from './components/Login';
import Signup from './components/Signup';

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <Header />
      <Switch>
        <Route exact path="/" component={Home} />
        <Route path="/courses" component={Courses} />
        <Route path="/lessons" component={Lessons} />
        <Route path="/login" component={Login} />
        <Route path="/signup" component={Signup} />
        {/* You can add more routes as needed */}
      </Switch>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

7. Wrap your `App` component with the `Router` component in `index.js`:

```javascript
ReactDOM.render(
  <React.StrictMode>
    <Router>
      <App />
    </Router>
  </React.StrictMode>,
  document.getElementById('root')
);
```

