This is a function or template parameter for a C++ function or class template that expects a reference to a `const` array of `WIDECHAR` (wide character) literals of fixed size `N`.

Here's what each component of this parameter type means:

- `const` - indicates that the array of wide character literals is constant and cannot be modified inside the function or class using the parameter.
- `WIDECHAR` - denotes the character type of the array of wide character literals. This is typically either `wchar_t` or `char16_t` depending on the encoding being used.
- `(&Literal)` - indicates that the parameter is a reference to the array of wide character literals. This is used so that the function or class can access the parameter without making a copy of the array.
- `[N]` - specifies the size of the array of wide character literals. The `N` in this case is a template parameter, meaning that it can be any compile-time constant value passed as a template parameter when the function or class template is instantiated. This allows for flexibility in the size of the array of wide character literals that can be passed to the function or class.

Here's an example usage of `const WIDECHAR(&Literal)[N]` as a function parameter:

```c++
#include <iostream>

template <typename WIDECHAR, size_t N>
void printWideCharArray(const WIDECHAR(&arr)[N]) {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < N; i++) {
        std::wcout << arr[i];
    }
    std::wcout << std::endl;
}

int main() {
    const wchar_t wideCharArr[5] = {'H', 'e', 'l', 'l', 'o'};
    printWideCharArray(wideCharArr);

    const char16_t utf16Arr[11] = {0x0053, 0x0069, 0x006D, 0x0070, 0x006C, 0x0065, 0x0074, 0x0065, 0x0073, 0x0074, 0x00E1};
    printWideCharArray(utf16Arr);

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, we've defined a function template called `printWideCharArray` that takes a reference to a constant array of wide characters of a fixed size `N`. Then we've demonstrated how this function can be instantiated with different `N` and different wide character types.

Yes, a static member function of a template class is associated with the class itself, and is not dependent on the template arguments used to instantiate the class. This means that there is only one version of the static function across all instances of the class, regardless of their template arguments.

For example:

```c++
#include <iostream>

template<typename T>
class MyClass {
public:
    static void staticFunction() {
        std::cout << "Static function called" << std::endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    MyClass<int>::staticFunction();   // outputs "Static function called"
    MyClass<double>::staticFunction();// outputs "Static function called"

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, we have a template class called `MyClass` that has a static member function called `staticFunction()`. When we call the function on two different instantiations of the class (`MyClass<int>` and `MyClass<double>`), the same message is printed to the console. This shows that there is only one version of the static function across all instances of `MyClass`. 

Note that if you want to specialize the implementation of a static member function for a specific template argument, you can do so using explicit template specialization or partial template specialization. In this case, the specialized implementation will only be used for that specific template argument, and not for any other instantiations of the template.

