You can use the QPainter class in conjunction with QPixmap to draw the source QPixmap onto the destination QPixmap at the specified location. Here's an example function that demonstrates how to copy a QPixmap image to another QPixmap image at a specific location:

```cpp
#include <QPixmap>
#include <QPainter>

void copyPixmapAtLocation(QPixmap &dst, const QPixmap &src, int x, int y)
{
    QPainter painter(&dst);        // Start painting on the destination QPixmap
    painter.drawPixmap(x, y, src); // Draw the source QPixmap at the specified location (x, y)
    painter.end();                 // End painting
}
```

Usage:

```cpp
int main()
{
    QPixmap srcPixmap("source_image.png");        // Load the source QPixmap
    QPixmap dstPixmap(500, 500);                  // Create the destination QPixmap with a specific size
    dstPixmap.fill(Qt::white);                    // Fill the destination QPixmap with a background color

    int x = 100;                                  // X coordinate for the location
    int y = 100;                                  // Y coordinate for the location

    copyPixmapAtLocation(dstPixmap, srcPixmap, x, y);

    dstPixmap.save("destination_image.png");      // Save the resulting QPixmap to a file

    return 0;
}
```

In the example above, the `copyPixmapAtLocation()` function takes the destination QPixmap (dst), source QPixmap (src), and the target location (x and y) as arguments. The QPainter object is used to draw the source QPixmap onto the destination QPixmap at the specified location.

